Runtime dependency — WebUSB fastboot transport (declared + exactly pinned in
`package.json` `dependencies`, so a clean `npm install` / `npm ci` resolves it;
T-WEBINSTALL-WEBUSB-TRANSPORT-BUNDLE, D-043):

  android-fastboot (kdrag0n/fastboot.js)  MIT
    pinned to commit ffe7e270061b95fe0f0f3abd1aa7d3c28999d1d9
    git+https://github.com/kdrag0n/fastboot.js.git#<that commit>
  @zip.js/zip.js 2.24.0                   BSD-3-Clause  (lockfile-pinned transitive)
  pako 2.2.0                              MIT AND Zlib  (lockfile-pinned transitive)

The published npm version 1.1.3 (2023-01-19) PREDATES that commit (2024-08-06),
so the pin is a commit, not a semver range: an exact-version pin could not
satisfy the "pin ≥ ffe7e270" requirement. The upstream package declares its own
transitives as ranges (`^2.7.6`, `^2.1.0`); the resolved versions above are
pinned in `package-lock.json`. npm records an integrity hash for the git
dependency but does **not** verify it on install (npm warns "skipping integrity
check for git dependency"), so the trust anchor is the commit SHA.

`connectAndroidFastboot()` is the real adapter (loads the pinned package
lazily, connects, wraps the device in the existing `FastbootTransport`). The
wizard does **not** call it yet: `wizard/main.ts` still uses
`readOptionalFastbootDevice()` + `adaptAndroidFastboot` with a runtime-supplied
`FastbootDevice`, the dry-run transport remains the default, and DEC-009 keeps
live `executePlan`/`lock` on HOLD. The static wizard build has no bundler step,
so a bare `android-fastboot` ESM specifier would not resolve in the browser
until a separate bundling card lands. Dry-run / plan preview never load it.

Wizard UI (`wizard/index.html`, `wizard/styles.css`, `wizard/main.ts`) is
original GuardTalkOS work. It does not copy GrapheneOS HTML, CSS, JavaScript,
or marketing copy.

WebUSB ADB reboot (`wizard/adb-*.ts`) is original GuardTalkOS code. It speaks
the public ADB USB protocol (`reboot:bootloader`) and does not bundle a third
party ADB library. It is not a live flash path.

Key-at-rest disclosure: the wizard persists its ADB host key as a full RSA
private JWK (including `d`) **unencrypted in `localStorage`** under
`guardtalk.webadb.rsa-jwk`. Any script in the wizard origin or anyone with
access to the browser profile can read it; exposure is limited to ADB
debugging trust. Clear it with
`localStorage.removeItem("guardtalk.webadb.rsa-jwk")` (see README,
"Browser ADB key storage").

SHA-256 in flashcore is a portable implementation (`src/sha256-portable.ts`)
so the wizard can verify artifacts in the browser without `node:crypto`.

